From: Hunter B Hough <hunter.hough@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 16:48:01 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 96/99] fix(refinery): detect already-merged by content, not
 only ancestry (gcp-a4e7)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

mol-refinery-patrol's merge-state gate had a single already-merged arm,
`git merge-base --is-ancestor origin/$BRANCH origin/$TARGET`. That arm is
essentially unreachable on this lane: the merge script rebases `temp` onto
the target and ff-merges, which rewrites every commit, so after a successful
merge the pre-rebase branch sha is nowhere on the target.

The consequence was the exact outcome the arm exists to prevent. A crash
between `git push` and `gc bd close` fell through to the false-completion
guard, which correctly reported 0 commits / 0 diff (the rebase drops the
already-applied commit) and halted a genuinely merged bead: status blocked,
merge_result=refused_false_completion, gc.routed_to=human, mayor and witness
paged. Live case winnow-zgr2y.6.

Ancestry is a sufficient condition for already-merged, never a necessary one,
so add a second arm on the axis that survives a rewrite:

- `branch_already_landed` asks `git cherry` whether every commit the branch
  introduced since its recorded `fork_sha` is already upstream by patch-id.
  It fires only when the branch also has a non-empty diff against its own
  fork point and carries a commit the target lacks, which is what keeps it
  from swallowing the two halts it sits next to: the starved 0-commit polecat,
  and the branch merely rebased onto newer target whose counted commits belong
  to other beads (gcp-duy's false positive, on the reachability axis).
- `upstream_equivalent_sha` names the commit ON the target that carries the
  work, so the rebase-aware close records a `merged_sha` that is actually
  reachable from `merged_target` — the pre-rebase branch tip is not.
- `close_already_merged` is now shared by both arms, so they cannot drift on
  what a merged close records, and stamps `already_merged_via` (ancestor |
  rebase_patch_id) plus `merged_source_sha`.

Without `fork_sha` recorded neither arm can fire; the gate stays conservative
and falls through to the halt, exactly as before.

The helper group and the gate are now sentinel-delimited so the shipped text
is executable by tests rather than transcribed. gastown/tests/test_refinery_
already_merged.sh extracts both and runs them against real repositories with a
`gc` stub: a crash after a REBASING merge (the shape that reproduces the
defect — an ff-merge fixture goes green against the unfixed gate), the ancestor
arm a fast-forward rig still needs, both halts, a missing fork_sha, and an
ordinary merge candidate falling through. Verified red on the pre-fix gate
(6 failures, including "was halted as a false completion") and green after.

